### Book CRUD
### Start PostgreSQL first (`docker compose up -d` from 02-bookstore-capstone/), then
### `./mvnw spring-boot:run`, then execute these top to bottom.
### Every endpoint is public at this stage; Step 3 adds JWT and the ADMIN role.

@host = http://localhost:8080

### 1. List books (paged, seeded with 5 rows under the dev profile)
GET {{host}}/api/books

### 2. List with paging and sorting
GET {{host}}/api/books?page=0&size=2&sort=price,desc

### 3. Keyword search over the title
GET {{host}}/api/books?keyword=java

### 4. Get one book -> 200
GET {{host}}/api/books/1

### 5. Get a missing book -> 404 with the ErrorResponse envelope
GET {{host}}/api/books/9999

### 6. Create a book with an author -> 201 + Location header
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Refactoring",
  "isbn": "9780134757599",
  "price": 47.99,
  "stock": 10,
  "authorId": 2
}

### 6b. authorId is optional — a book with no author on record -> 201
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Anonymous Manual",
  "isbn": "9999999999999",
  "price": 9.99,
  "stock": 1
}

### 6c. A non-existent authorId is a client error, not a silently-dropped field -> 404
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Ghost",
  "isbn": "8888888888888",
  "price": 9.99,
  "stock": 1,
  "authorId": 9999
}

### 7. Create with a duplicate ISBN -> 409
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Refactoring (again)",
  "isbn": "9780134757599",
  "price": 47.99,
  "stock": 1
}

### 8. Create with invalid fields -> 400 with per-field messages
POST {{host}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "",
  "price": -5,
  "stock": -1
}

### 9. Update a book -> 200
PUT {{host}}/api/books/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Clean Code (2nd printing)",
  "isbn": "9780132350884",
  "price": 39.99,
  "stock": 20
}

### 10. Update a missing book -> 404
PUT {{host}}/api/books/9999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Ghost",
  "price": 10.00,
  "stock": 1
}

### 11. Delete a book -> 204 No Content
DELETE {{host}}/api/books/5

### 12. Delete it again -> 404
DELETE {{host}}/api/books/5

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 2c — the N+1 demonstration
### Watch the application log with SQL logging on (dev profile) while running
### these two. Identical responses, very different query counts.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 13. Authors with their books — one LEFT JOIN FETCH -> 1 query
GET {{host}}/api/authors

### 14. The same data, the naive way -> 1 + N queries (6 with the seed data)
GET {{host}}/api/authors?naive=true

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 2e — purchase and optimistic locking
### For the concurrency demo run: node scripts/concurrent-purchase.mjs 30 30
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 15. Buy 2 copies -> 200, stock decremented
POST {{host}}/api/books/1/purchase
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 2 }

### 16. Buy more than the shelf holds -> 409 with the exact numbers
POST {{host}}/api/books/1/purchase
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 99999 }

### 17. A non-positive quantity is a validation error -> 400
POST {{host}}/api/books/1/purchase
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 0 }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 3a — registration
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 18. Register a customer -> 201. The response never contains the hash.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "ruoyu",
  "email": "ruoyu@example.com",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery"
}

### 19. Same username again -> 409
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "ruoyu",
  "email": "different@example.com",
  "password": "another-password"
}

### 20. Invalid input -> 400 listing every field that failed
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "ab",
  "email": "not-an-email",
  "password": "short"
}

### 21. role is not accepted from the client — this registers as USER, not ADMIN
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "wannabe-admin",
  "email": "wannabe@example.com",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery",
  "role": "ADMIN"
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 3b — login and JWT
### Run 22 first; the @token variable is captured from its response.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 22. Log in -> 200 with a signed token
# @name login
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "ruoyu",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery"
}

@token = {{login.response.body.token}}

### 23. The current user, identified by the token -> 200
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 24. Same endpoint without a token -> 401
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me

### 25. A token that is not one -> 401
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer not.a.token

### 26. Wrong password -> 401, deliberately the same message as 27
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "ruoyu",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### 27. A username that does not exist -> 401, byte-identical to 26
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "nobody-here",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 3c — role-based authorization
### Run 28 and 29 to capture both tokens, then compare 30-33.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 28. Log in as a customer
# @name userLogin
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "ruoyu", "password": "correct-horse-battery" }

@userToken = {{userLogin.response.body.token}}

### 29. Log in as the seeded dev admin
# @name adminLogin
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "admin", "password": "admin-dev-password" }

@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.token}}

### 30. Catalog reads stay public — no token at all -> 200
GET {{host}}/api/books

### 31. A customer creating a book -> 403 (authenticated, but not permitted)
POST {{host}}/api/books
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Not Allowed", "isbn": "NOPE-1", "price": 9.99, "stock": 1 }

### 32. The same request as admin -> 201
POST {{host}}/api/books
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Admin Only Book", "isbn": "ADM-1", "price": 9.99, "stock": 1 }

### 33. Buying requires an account, either role -> 200
POST {{host}}/api/books/1/purchase
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 1 }

### 34. An undeclared route is closed by default -> 401
GET {{host}}/api/whatever
